# Chapter 1: The Letter (ticket 0707; reworked scene by scene in 0728).
# Beat sheet: docs/story/chapters/ch01.md. Continuity: docs/story/ledger.md.
#
# Scene ids, as chapters/ch01.ron and battles/ch01.ron wire them (0803):
#
#   Before the battle (intro_scenes, in this order)
#     ch01_intro                 the opening caption, then the letter
#     ch01_prebattle             the Red Company; the party gathers
#
#   In the battle (triggers, each once)
#     ch01_first_turn            TurnStart(turn: 1, phase: Player)
#     ch01_boss_engage_lead      CombatStart(unit: "red_captain", against: Some("lead"))
#     ch01_boss_engage_sergeant  CombatStart(unit: "red_captain", against: Some("sergeant"))
#     ch01_boss_engage           CombatStart(unit: "red_captain"): anyone else
#     ch01_boss_half             HalfHp(unit: "red_captain")
#     ch01_death_red_captain     UnitFell(unit: "red_captain")
#     ch01_death_lead            UnitFell(unit: "lead"): game over in both modes
#     ch01_death_<id>            UnitFell(unit: "<id>", mode: Some(Classic))
#     ch01_retreat_<id>          UnitFell(unit: "<id>", mode: Some(Casual))
#       for <id> = retainer, sergeant, poacher, keeper, heretic
#
#   After the battle (victory_scenes, in this order)
#     ch01_victory
#     ch01_tbc
#
# The lead has three reply choices: one each in ch01_intro, ch01_prebattle
# and ch01_victory. Aske counts fourteen men in the fort, three of them
# with bows: the map's enemy count should not contradict it (chapter-1.md:
# thirteen soldiers, three of them Archers, and the boss).
#
# The title cinematic (assets/cinematics/title.ron) shows the first three
# boxes of ch01_intro (the opening captions) and the first box of
# ch01_prebattle (Tamsin's news). Keep those boxes first.
#
# Tone (0724, 0728; docs/design/setting-and-tone.md, "Where the jokes go"):
# the opening and the battle are played straight. Nobody jokes in
# ch01_intro, ch01_prebattle or a battle scene, except briefly in answer to
# the lead's wry reply. Serious is not flat: a line gives its speaker's
# angle on a fact, not only the fact. ch01_victory has one light moment
# (the eleven arrows).
#
# A companion who fell in Classic is gone (0716; @if blocks, 0715,
# ADR-0055). ch01_victory plays without any of the five, and without all
# of them: the order's seal, that only the new lord may use it, and the
# road to the hall reach the player on every path. Read it with one
# companion gone, and with only the lead left, before changing it.
# docs/story/ledger.md ("If a companion died in Chapter 1") records what
# each death leaves unsaid.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Before the battle
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Hollis has had the letter in his coat all night. He drilled the lead at
# dawn anyway, because it is what they do, and because he wanted one more
# ordinary morning before he handed it over. The lead disarms him for the
# first time on the worst morning in four years.

@scene ch01_intro
@music village_home
> Three years after {n:event.ash_peace}.
> The war is won. The soldiers who won it were never paid.
> In {n:place.thornmarch}, a long way from any court, an exile chops wood.
@caption {N:place.harrowby}, {n:place.thornmarch}
# The yard behind the rented house, at dawn (harrowby_yard.map).
@place harrowby_yard 12,8
@left  lead neutral
@right retainer neutral
> Dawn, in the yard behind a rented house. Two practice swords crack
  together, wood on wood, as they have every morning for four years.
retainer: Guard up, {n:family.veyne}. Higher. It drops when you're tired.
  Don't let it.
lead: Again.
> Three strokes. On the fourth, the old man's sword is lying in the dirt
  between them.
retainer[surprised]: ...Good. That's good. Four years I've been waiting for
  you to do that.
retainer[sad]: Leave it where it is. Sit down, kid. There's something in my
  coat that I've spent all morning not giving you.
retainer: A peddler left it last night. No seal, no name. It didn't need
  one. I'd know that handwriting anywhere, and so will you.
> "Leave the march. Tonight. They are coming for everyone who was there
  that night. Burn this."
lead: {n:rival.first}.
retainer: Four years. Not a line to you. Not one to his own father. And
  when he finally writes, it's that.
@choice
* earnest: He's warning us. He still cares, {n:retainer.first}.
  retainer[sad]: Aye. That's what worries me.
* wry: Four years of silence, and now he's urgent.
  retainer[happy]: Ha. He never did write unless he needed something.
  retainer[sad]: I'd take four more years of quiet over this.
* blunt: What does he want?
  retainer[angry]: Us gone, by the look of it. What I'd ask him is who's
    coming. And how he knows.
@endchoice
retainer[sad]: "Everyone who was there." That's you. That's me. That's
  him. And my girl, and she's past anyone coming for her.
> He does not burn the letter. He folds it small, and puts it back inside
  his coat.
> Hooves on the ford road, coming hard.
retainer[neutral]: That's the sergeant. She doesn't ride like that for good
  news. Get your coat.
@end

# The green. Everyone comes in with the thing they noticed: Tamsin the
# price and the thumb over the seal, Maud the words "burned hands", Rue
# the eyes on her, Aske the arrows and the deer. Hollis says nothing until
# he has steel in his hands.

@scene ch01_prebattle
@caption The green, {n:place.harrowby}
# Harrowby by the chapel: the green where the party starts (ch01.map).
@place battle 3,12
@music dungeon_tense
@left  retainer neutral
@right sergeant angry
sergeant: Captain. Your Former Grace. {N:red_captain.nickname} is in the
  toll-fort with the whole company. I rode up to the gate to ask him what
  he wanted here. He asked me if I wanted my old job back.
@left keeper surprised
keeper: Sergeant? We heard the horse from the chapel. Nobody rides onto the
  green like that unless someone is hurt. I've brought bandages, and
  {n:heretic} to carry the rest.
sergeant[neutral]: Nobody's hurt yet, Keeper. He showed me a paper with a
  lord's seal on it. His thumb stayed over the seal the whole time I was
  reading.
sergeant: Three people on it, and a price by each that would feed his
  company till spring. No names. "The exile, the old man, and the girl
  with the burned hands."
keeper: Burned... but I only asked them to... Oh. Oh, {n:god.mother.name}.
@right heretic angry
heretic: What? What's everyone looking at? They're hands. And don't you
  stare, heir. It's you at the top of that paper, not me.
> {N:retainer.first} has not said a word. He turns, and walks back toward
  the house.
> Something drops out of the chapel yew: a thin girl, a long bow, two
  rabbits.
@left  poacher neutral
@right sergeant neutral
poacher: Fourteen in the fort. I counted at dusk and again at first light.
  Three have bows. Last night they shot at the deer from the wall. Many
  arrows. Most of them hit nothing.
sergeant: Fourteen is what I counted from the gate, too. Why bring it to
  us, {n:faction.brennmark.adj}?
poacher[angry]: Not to you. They killed three deer and carried off one. The
  other two are lying in my woods for the crows. I will help you, noble.
  Once. For the deer.
@left  keeper sad
@right heretic angry
keeper: {N:heretic}. The chapel cellar bars from the inside. Please go
  down. It is you they have come for.
heretic: No. I've hidden in enough cellars. I'm sworn to fire, Keeper, and
  you know it. I can burn them. I'm fighting.
@left  lead neutral
@right retainer neutral
> {N:retainer.first} comes back across the green with a long bundle of
  oilcloth. It has been under the floorboards since the day they came.
retainer: Four years of wooden swords. This one cuts. So will theirs.
> {lead} takes the sword. The terms of {their} exile forbid it.
retainer[sad]: That paper has three of us on it. Between here and that
  fort there are forty people who aren't on any paper. Your call,
  {n:family.veyne}.
@choice
* earnest: We keep them away from the village.
  @right poacher neutral
  poacher: The miller's wife buys my rabbits. She pays me too much and
    pretends she cannot count. Keep them away from her.
  @right sergeant neutral
* wry: Only fourteen? I'm insulted.
  @right sergeant happy
  sergeant: Ha! Somebody had to say it.
  sergeant[neutral]: Don't be. I rode with them. They were good soldiers
    once, and {n:red_captain.first} still is.
* blunt: We hit the fort first.
  @right heretic angry
  heretic: Giving orders already, heir? ...Fine. It's the right one.
  @right sergeant neutral
@endchoice
sergeant[neutral]: {N:red_captain.first} won't leave with that paper
  unpaid. Any of them who gets away comes back tonight with torches. So it
  has to be all of them.
lead: Let's move.
@end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# In the battle
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

@scene ch01_first_turn
@left retainer neutral
retainer: Stay close, kid. The first real one's always ugly. Guard up, and
  let them come to us.
@end

# Harl's first fight. A scene written for him and one character replaces
# the general one (docs/design/battle-scenes-and-recruitment.md). The
# general one meets Hollis and Rue, who are on his paper, and Aske and
# Maud, who aren't.

@scene ch01_boss_engage
@right red_captain neutral
red_captain: Three heads on my paper, three hundred crowns a head. If yours
  isn't one of them, you're dying for nothing.
red_captain[angry]: The war taught me not to mind which. Come on, then.
@end

@scene ch01_boss_engage_lead
@left  lead neutral
@right red_captain happy
red_captain: Nothing personal, Your Grace. You're three hundred crowns and a
  hot meal for my men.
lead: Who's paying?
red_captain[neutral]: Ask me when I'm dead.
@end

@scene ch01_boss_engage_sergeant
@left  sergeant neutral
@right red_captain angry
red_captain: {n:sergeant.last}. You left before payday. Bad habit.
sergeant[angry]: I left after the farm, {n:red_captain.first}. You know I
  did.
red_captain: A lord paid for that farm. A lord's paying for this. Somebody
  has to pay for something.
sergeant[sad]: Then it's your bill, {n:red_captain.first}. I stopped signing
  for it last winter.
@end

@scene ch01_boss_half
@right red_captain angry
red_captain: Hah. I bled worse than this at the passes, for a king who never
  learned my name.
red_captain: I'll not die owed. Not here. Not by you.
@end

# Said across the field: the lead may be nowhere near him.

@scene ch01_death_red_captain
@right red_captain sad
red_captain: Hah... Still owed. Figures.
red_captain: Your Grace! One thing, free of charge. The new lord of
  {n:family.veyne} pays in good silver.
red_captain: Your own crest on the order, and everything. Must be nice...
  having friends.
@end

# The lead's fall is game over in both modes.

@scene ch01_death_lead
@left lead sad
> The letter said to run. {lead} stayed.
@end

# The companions: a death quote in Classic, a retreat line in Casual
# (docs/design/death-and-difficulty.md). They can fire in any order, so
# none of them speaks to another companion, and what one says of another
# (the keeper's "the one it hurt", the keeper's stove) holds whether or not
# that one has already fallen.

@scene ch01_death_retainer
@left retainer sad
retainer: Hnh. I'm down, kid. Don't stop for me.
retainer: Eyes front, {lead}. And if you ever see the boy again, tell
  him... No. Leave it. He knows.
@end

@scene ch01_retreat_retainer
@left retainer angry
retainer: Hnh. That's my leg. I'm no good to you in the line on one leg.
retainer[neutral]: Guard up, {n:family.veyne}. Finish it, then come and
  find me at the house.
@end

@scene ch01_death_sergeant
@left sergeant sad
sergeant: Ah. That'll cost us.
sergeant: Take the ledger off my belt, Your Former Grace. Four thousand, two
  hundred and twelve crowns. Don't let them off a single one.
@end

@scene ch01_retreat_sergeant
@left sergeant angry
sergeant: That's a hole in me. I can't keep the saddle with it.
sergeant[neutral]: I'm out, Your Former Grace. Don't let one of them walk
  away.
@end

@scene ch01_death_poacher
@left poacher surprised
poacher: I counted wrong. There was one more.
poacher[sad]: My silver is in my left boot. Send it north, noble. To my
  {n:word.mother}. You owe me that.
@end

@scene ch01_retreat_poacher
@left poacher angry
poacher: I am hit. It is not deep. But I cannot draw the bow with this arm.
poacher: I go back to the mill. Do not lose, noble.
@end

@scene ch01_death_keeper
@left keeper surprised
keeper: Oh. Oh... I can't close this one. Not on myself.
keeper[sad]: {N:god.mother.name} keep you all. I did a thing this autumn,
  and I am sorry for it. I had hoped to say so myself, to the one it hurt.
@end

@scene ch01_retreat_keeper
@left keeper surprised
keeper: I'm hurt. I... oh. I can hardly stand, and I can't mend anyone
  like this.
keeper[sad]: I must go back. I'm sorry. {N:god.mother.name} keep you all.
@end

@scene ch01_death_heretic
@left heretic angry
heretic: {N:god.pyre.name}'s teeth. Not now. I only just stopped being
  hungry.
heretic[sad]: Don't stare, heir. The Keeper's stove was warm. I should have
  told her so.
@end

@scene ch01_retreat_heretic
@left heretic angry
heretic: {N:god.pyre.name}'s teeth! I can't hold the fire. It's shaking in
  my hands.
heretic: Finish them, heir. All of them.
@end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# After the battle
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

@scene ch01_victory
@caption The toll-fort, noon
# The toll-fort, just below its keep at (20, 2).
@place battle 20,3
@music scene_sad
> Across the river, one barn smokes at the edge of {n:place.harrowby}. Only
  the one.
@left lead neutral

# Over the body. Without the sergeant, the Captain finds the orders;
# without him too, the lead does.
@if sergeant
@right sergeant neutral
sergeant: Payday, {n:red_captain.first}. You go in the ledger with the rest
  of them. Paid in full.
> She doesn't smile. She crouches, closes his eyes with two fingers, and
  takes a folded paper from inside his vest.
sergeant[sad]: He taught me to ride. ...It was a job. That's all it was.
@right clear
@else
> {N:red_captain} lies where he fell. {N:sergeant.first}'s ledger is in
  {lead}'s coat now, and nobody is left to mark him paid.
@if retainer
@right retainer sad
> {N:retainer.first} gets down beside the body, one knee at a time, and
  takes a folded paper from inside the vest.
retainer: The sergeant would have had a word for him. I haven't.
@right clear
@else
> {lead} kneels, and takes a folded paper from inside the dead man's vest.
@endif
@endif
> His orders: three hundred crowns a head for the exile, the old man and
  the girl. The seal is blue wax. The crest in it is the one {lead} cut from
  {their} own coat four years ago.

# Whose seal it is now. The keeper knows the law; without her the sergeant
# works it out; without both, nobody says it.
@if sergeant
@right sergeant neutral
sergeant: He wasn't lying, then. That's your crest, Your Former Grace. I
  thought they took it off you with the house.
@right clear
@endif
@if keeper
@left keeper sad
@if retainer
@if sergeant
@right sergeant neutral
keeper: They did. The seal goes with the title. Nobody may use it but the
  lord of {n:family.veyne}, and the lord of {n:family.veyne}, these three
  years... Captain, I am sorry.
@right clear
@else
keeper: The seal goes with the title, and the title was taken. Nobody may
  use it but the lord of {n:family.veyne}, and for three years that has
  been... Captain, I am sorry.
@endif
@else
keeper: The seal goes with the title, and the title was taken. Nobody may
  use it but the lord of {n:family.veyne}, and for three years that has
  been... Oh, {n:god.mother.name} keep the Captain.
@endif
@left lead neutral
@else
@if sergeant
@right sergeant sad
sergeant: They did. A seal goes where the title goes. One man in the kingdom
  may press that into wax now, and it isn't you.
@right clear
@else
> The seal went with the title. For three years, one man alone has had the
  right to press it into wax.
@endif
@endif

# The name. The sergeant says it; without her, nobody does.
@if retainer
@if sergeant
> Everyone looks at {n:retainer.first}. {N:retainer.first} looks at the wax,
  and says nothing at all.
@right sergeant neutral
sergeant: {N:rival}, hero of the war. The Captain's boy. Somebody say
  something. I'm no good in a quiet.
@right clear
@else
> {N:rival}, lord of {n:family.veyne}, hero of the war. Nobody says the
  name. {N:retainer.first} looks at the wax, and says nothing at all.
@endif
@else
@if sergeant
@right sergeant sad
sergeant: {N:rival}, hero of the war. The Captain's boy. And the Captain
  went down this morning without having to see it. Somebody say something.
  I'm no good in a quiet.
@right clear
@else
> {N:rival}, lord of {n:family.veyne}, hero of the war:
  {n:retainer.first}'s son. {N:retainer.first} died this morning without
  having to see it.
@endif
@endif

# The cart is hers alone to tell: without her it is not said (ledger).
@if heretic
@right heretic neutral
@if sergeant
heretic: I'll say something. I've seen that seal before, heir. On a cart. At
  night. With children in it.
@else
heretic: I've seen that seal before, heir. On a cart. At night. With
  children in it.
@endif
lead: Where?
heretic[angry]: You tell me. Four years ago it was your family's to stamp.
@right clear
@endif
@if keeper
@left keeper sad
@if heretic
@right heretic angry
keeper: Please. Not here. Somebody was told that {n:heretic} is in
  {n:place.harrowby}, and only {n:faction.vigil} hunts a girl for her hands.
  They will send others.
@right clear
@else
keeper: {N:heretic} slept by my stove all autumn, and somebody was told so.
  I... somebody was told. They will hear that it is not finished here, and
  they will send others.
@endif
@left lead neutral
@endif

# Who comes along.
@if poacher
@left poacher neutral
@if keeper
poacher: Then I come also. You owe me eleven arrows, noble.
@else
poacher: Someone paid for these men. He will pay for more. I come with
  you. You owe me eleven arrows, noble.
@endif
@left lead neutral
@endif
@if heretic
@right heretic neutral
heretic: I'm not following you, heir. I'm walking behind you. Where I can
  see your hands.
@right clear
@endif
@if sergeant
@right sergeant neutral
sergeant: And I'll ride along as far as there's pay. ...Further, probably.
@right clear
@endif

# The Captain asks; the lead answers. He stays on screen while the choice
# is open, so the choice is written once with him and once without. With
# him, he names the day's dead before he buries anyone.
@if retainer
@right retainer sad
retainer: The boy's at {n:place.veyne_hall}, kid. One step off the march to
  ask him and you're an outlaw, for good. Say the word anyway.
@choice
* earnest: He'll tell me why. I have to believe that.
  retainer: Aye. I'd like to believe it too.
* wry: I've always wanted to be an outlaw.
  @if sergeant
  @right sergeant happy
  sergeant: It's soldiering with worse food and better company. You'll take
    to it.
  @right retainer sad
  @else
  retainer: That one was for the sergeant. She'd have had an answer.
  @endif
* blunt: {N:place.veyne_hall}. We leave now.
  @if poacher
  @right poacher neutral
  poacher: Good. This fort smells of them.
  @right retainer sad
  @else
  retainer: Aye. That's the word.
  @endif
@endchoice
@if sergeant
@else
retainer[sad]: The sergeant goes in the chapel yard, in the red jacket.
  Somebody see to her horse. It's still waiting on her.
@endif
@if keeper
@else
retainer[sad]: The keeper goes by her own chapel wall. There's nobody left
  who has the words, so I'll dig it deep. It's what I've got.
@endif
@if heretic
@else
retainer[sad]: The girl goes in the yard with her hands wrapped. She was on
  that paper next to me, and I never once asked her why.
@endif
@if poacher
@else
retainer[sad]: {N:poacher} faces north. She'd want that. Her silver's yours
  to send, kid. Every coin of it.
@endif
retainer[neutral]: Go and pack. I'll find a shovel. I've buried better men
  than {n:red_captain}. I'll dig this one's hole myself. It's the least I
  can do for him.
@right clear
@else
> {N:rival.first} is at {n:place.veyne_hall}. One step off the march to ask
  him why, and {lead} is an outlaw for good. {N:retainer.first} would have
  said so. Then he would have said: go.
@choice
* earnest: He'll tell me why. I have to believe that.
  > Nobody answers. {N:retainer.first} would have.
* wry: I've always wanted to be an outlaw.
  @if sergeant
  @right sergeant sad
  sergeant: It's soldiering with worse food. The company was better
    yesterday.
  @right clear
  @else
  > It would have gotten a laugh, yesterday.
  @endif
* blunt: {N:place.veyne_hall}. We leave now.
  @if poacher
  @right poacher neutral
  poacher: Yes. Now. This fort smells of them.
  @right clear
  @else
  > Nobody argues.
  @endif
@endchoice
> {lead} digs {n:retainer.first}'s grave {themself}, and takes the letter
  from inside his coat. {They} does not burn it either. Nobody digs a hole
  for {n:red_captain}.
@if sergeant
@else
> {N:sergeant.first} is buried in her one red jacket. Her horse stands
  saddled at the gate all afternoon, waiting to be told where it is going.
@endif
@if keeper
@else
> {N:keeper} lies by her own chapel wall. Nobody says the words over her, or
  over any of them: she was the one who knew them.
@endif
@if heretic
@else
> {N:heretic} goes into the ground with her hands wrapped, under the only
  name anyone here knew her by. Whatever she knew about any of this, she
  kept.
@endif
@if poacher
@else
> {N:poacher}'s grave faces north. The silver from her left boot is in
  {lead}'s pack. It has a long way to go.
@endif
@endif
> By dusk {lead} is on the road out of {n:place.harrowby}. Past the boundary
  stone, every step {they} takes is against the king's law.
@end

@scene ch01_tbc
@caption {N:place.veyne_hall}
# The old lord's study, at night: the desk (veyne_study.map).
@place veyne_study 12,7
@music talk_antagonist
> Night. The old lord's study. The banner of {n:house.veyne} hangs where it
  always hung, over a man who was not born under it.
@left  rival neutral
@right messenger neutral
messenger: {N:vowmaster.title} asks whether it's done.
> {N:rival.first} reads the report on his desk again. Three lines.
  {N:faction.red_company} is dead, to a man. The exile is not.
rival: ...Tell him it's done.
@right clear
> The messenger goes. {N:rival.first} burns the report in the candle flame,
  then looks down for a long time at the blue sash across his chest, and
  the full crest of {n:house.veyne} on it.
rival[sad]: I told you to run, {n:family.veyne}.
@end
